_message": "The likely culprit is `lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org'`. I can only see the query, not the schema or an `EXPLAIN`, so this is a diagnosis from reading it, not a measurement.\n\n**Why it's slow**\n- **The function call defeats the index.** Wrapping the column in `lower()` means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used. The database has to scan every row in `users` and lowercase each email. That cost grows with the table, which fits a page that got slow over time.\n- **The join may also be unindexed.** If `orders.user_id` has no index, each matching user triggers a scan of `orders`. This is worth checking even if you fix the first problem.\n- **`SELECT *` returns more than needed.** It pulls every column from both tables, including wide ones, and it prevents index-only scans. This is usually a smaller factor.\n\n**Fixes, in order of preference**\n1. Add an expression index that matches the query exactly:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   This needs no app change. `CONCURRENTLY` is Postgres syntax. It avoids locking writes while the index builds.\n2. Confirm `orders.user_id` is indexed:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n   ```\n3. Select only the columns the page uses, such as `o.id, o.created_at, o.total`.\n4. For a longer-term fix, normalize emails to lowercase on write. Then you can use `u.email = ...` against a plain index, or a `citext` column.\n\n**What \"since last week\" suggests**\nA change in the query alone wouldn't explain a sudden slowdown. The more likely causes are a recent deploy that added the `lower()`, a dropped or never-created index, or table growth that crossed the point where the planner switched to a sequential scan. `git log` shows only \"Add exports\" here, so I can't tell which from the repo.\n\nTo confirm, run this against the database:\n```sql\nEXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT ... ;\n```\nLook for `Seq Scan on users` or `Seq Scan on orders`. If you paste the output, or the `users` and `orders` schemas with their indexes, I can say which fix matters most. I can also write the migration.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}